Claims:
[
    "The **MMLU benchmark**—short for *Massive Multitask Language Understanding*—is one of the foundational evaluations designed to test how well large language models (LLMs) grasp a broad range of knowledge and reasoning across multiple subjects.",
    "The intuition behind MMLU is to measure whether an LLM can handle *many* things *simultaneously* rather than just excelling in one narrow task.",
    "Unlike earlier benchmarks, MMLU was built to simulate the kind of diverse, real-world knowledge a general-purpose AI might need.",
    "The original version of MMLU covered **57 subjects**, spanning elementary math, high school biology, law, ethics, medicine, computer science, and other professional-level topics.",
    "Each subject in MMLU had a set of **multiple-choice questions**, providing a structured way to evaluate the model's understanding.",
    "MMLU questions were designed as multiple-choice with options provided to the model, helping standardize evaluation for precision and accuracy under constraints.",
    "The original MMLU had a different number of options per question (not 10, as may be recalled from other benchmarks).",
    "Over time, as models improved, MMLU became *too easy* for the next generation of LLMs, reaching a point called 'saturation' where most models could achieve near-perfect scores, reducing its usefulness.",
    "To address saturation, MMLU was refined into **MMLU Pro**, which adjusted difficulty by reducing the number of subjects from **57 to 12** and focusing on a more curated set.",
    "MMLU Pro increased the number of questions per subject to **1,000** (totaling **12,000 questions**), ensuring a robust test of knowledge.",
    "MMLU Pro introduced questions requiring *reasoning* beyond rote memorization, making the benchmark harder to 'game' by memorizing answers.",
    "MMLU was groundbreaking because it moved beyond single-domain evaluations (like testing only math or only medicine) to assess *multitasking* ability.",
